course: dictionary
lesson: 4757
title: Hourglass
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An hourglass is a timer. It has two glass bulbs joined by a narrow neck, and sand runs from the top bulb to the bottom in a set time. «An hourglass is a timer you can watch: sand falls through a narrow neck.» You can see time passing as the sand falls.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is which. In «an hourglass figure», there is no sand at all: the word describes a shape that is narrow in the middle and wide at the top and bottom. Ask yourself: does the sentence mention sand, time or a shape? That clue settles it.
explain: Hourglasses are not only for games. Maths and science use them to measure time, such as a one-minute or three-minute sand timer. Stories use them to show time running out, and board games often use small ones.
fact: An hourglass is a glass timer with two bulbs joined by a narrow neck.
fact: «Hourglasses became common in Europe about 700 years ago.»
fact: An hourglass shape is narrow in the middle and wide at the top and bottom.
fact: Phrases to know: hourglass shape and sand timer.
fact: «Some atoms are radioactive and slowly change into other atoms at a steady rate, like sand falling through an hourglass.» A steady rate means the same speed every time.
exercise: «An hourglass is a timer you can watch: sand falls through a narrow neck.» What does an hourglass measure? | Distance | Temperature | *Time | Weight
exercise: In «an hourglass figure», what does hourglass describe? | A game piece | *A shape | A sand timer | A time of day
exercise: What happens to the sand in an hourglass? | *It runs from the top bulb to the bottom in a set time | It stays still in the middle | It rises up the neck | It turns into water
test: Through which part does sand fall in an hourglass? | Handle | Base | Lid | *Narrow neck
test: Which shape is an hourglass shape? | A square box | A flat circle | *Narrow in the middle, wide at the top and bottom | A long straight line
exam: In «like sand falling through an hourglass», what does the hourglass help us picture? | *A steady, even rate of change | A sudden explosion | A fast race | A loud noise
